Subject: input-surface: the keys go through the typing engine

With a text field for ordinary text, the keys of a language layout and the
emoji go to tactus-typer, which commits each key at once, keeps a copy of the
text before the cursor, corrects a word when it is ended, starts sentences
with a capital and turns two spaces into a full stop. Password fields, the
terminal layout and keys with a latched modifier go the old way.

Also here:
- an answer to the text field (an empty input-method commit) when the typing
  engine asks for one: the browser tells its text only once its last word
  was answered;
- Backspace held: letters, then whole words;
- the keys under the keyboard: the emoji page, the microphone, which
  starts the phone's dictation (omamobile.voice), and Paste: a tap pastes
  the last copy with Shift and Insert, a hold opens Omarchy's clipboard
  list (omarchy-menu-clipboard), and the key is there only while
  tactus-clipboard finds something to paste;
- a swipe down puts the keyboard away, and a touch of the text field brings
  it back;
- the surface is as high as the page it shows and leaves the band above the
  keys to the application: its input region ends at the panel's top. The
  room it reserves is a little less than the panel is high (8 px on a
  screen 360 wide), so the window in front reaches under the panel's top and
  it is the application that shows past the panel's round corners, not the
  wallpaper behind it. A taller page takes its room only once it is on the
  screen, and a lower one gives it back at once, so the wallpaper doesn't
  show between the window and the keyboard while the page changes;
- the pages change at once, and the page follows the text field's purpose
  (letters, numbers, the terminal's keys) each time the keyboard comes up.
